## Article I — Name, Office, and Registered Agent

### Section 1.1 — Name

The name of the corporation is **{{ org_name }}** (the "Corporation").

### Section 1.2 — Principal Office

The principal office of the Corporation is located at {{ principal_address }}. The Board of Directors may change the principal office by resolution at any regular or special meeting.

### Section 1.3 — Registered Agent

The Corporation shall continuously maintain a registered agent and registered office in the State of {{ state }} as required by {{ incorp_statute }}.{% if registered_agent_name %} **{{ registered_agent_name }}** (a{{ "n" if registered_agent_type == "individual" else "" }} {{ registered_agent_type }}) serves as the Corporation's registered agent.{% else %} [Name and type of registered agent to be designated by the Board.]{% endif %} The Board of Directors may change the registered agent or registered office by filing the appropriate notice with the {{ state }} Secretary of State (or equivalent state filing office).

---

## Article II — Purpose

### Section 2.1 — General Purpose

The Corporation is organized exclusively for [charitable/scientific/educational — describe your organization's actual mission] purposes within the meaning of Section 501(c)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, as amended.

### Section 2.2 — Limitations

No part of the net earnings of the Corporation shall inure to the benefit of, or be distributable to, its directors, officers, or other private persons, except that the Corporation is authorized to pay reasonable compensation for services rendered and to make payments and distributions in furtherance of the purposes stated in Section 2.1. No substantial part of the activities of the Corporation shall be the carrying on of propaganda or otherwise attempting to influence legislation, and the Corporation shall not participate in or intervene in any political campaign on behalf of or in opposition to any candidate for public office. Notwithstanding any other provision of these Bylaws, the Corporation shall not carry on any activity not permitted to be carried on by a corporation exempt from federal income tax under Section 501(c)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code.

### Section 2.3 — Public Charity Status

The Corporation is not a private foundation within the meaning of Section 509(a) of the Internal Revenue Code. The Corporation is classified as a public charity under **{{ public_charity_classification }}**{% if irs_determination_date %}, as determined by the IRS in its Determination Letter dated {{ irs_determination_date }}{% endif %}. The Corporation shall operate so as to maintain its public charity status and meet the applicable public support test.

### Section 2.4 — Whistleblower Protection

The Corporation shall maintain a Whistleblower Protection Policy, as adopted by the Board, providing that any director, officer, employee, volunteer, or contractor who reports in good faith a suspected violation of law, regulation, or organizational policy shall be protected from retaliation.

---

## Article III — Members

### Section 3.1 — No Statutory Members

[Adjust this Article if your organization has a voting membership structure.] The Corporation has no members within the meaning of {{ incorp_statute }}. All rights, powers, and duties that would otherwise be vested in the members are vested in the Board of Directors. Any action that would otherwise require the approval or vote of a majority of all members or the members with voting rights shall require only the approval of the Board of Directors.

---

## Article IV — Board of Directors

### Section 4.1 — General Powers

The business and affairs of the Corporation shall be managed by its Board of Directors. The Board may exercise all powers of the Corporation and do all lawful acts that are not by statute, the Articles of Incorporation, or these Bylaws directed or required to be exercised or done by others.

### Section 4.2 — Number

The Board of Directors shall consist of no fewer than **{{ board_size }}** and no more than **[maximum number]** directors. The exact number shall be fixed by resolution of the Board from time to time.

### Section 4.3 — Terms

Directors shall serve **[term length]**-year terms. [Specify whether terms auto-renew, are staggered, or are subject to re-election, and the notice required for non-renewal or removal.]

### Section 4.4 — Qualifications

Directors must be natural persons of at least eighteen (18) years of age. [State any residency or other qualification requirements, or state that none apply.]

### Section 4.5 — Nominations

[Describe your organization's process for identifying, evaluating, and recommending candidates for Board membership — e.g., via a Governance or Board Development Committee.] Elections shall be decided by majority vote of the directors present at a meeting where quorum is established, unless these Bylaws specify a higher threshold for a particular action.

### Section 4.6 — Vacancies

A vacancy on the Board — whether caused by death, resignation, removal, failure to renew, increase in the number of directors, or otherwise — shall be filled by a majority vote of the remaining directors, even if fewer than a quorum. A director elected to fill a vacancy shall serve for the unexpired term of the predecessor, or, in the case of a vacancy created by an increase in the number of directors, for a full term as described in Section 4.3.

### Section 4.7 — Removal

A director may be removed with or without cause by {{ amend_quorum_prose }} (excluding the director subject to removal from both the vote and the quorum count). Removal is effective upon passage of the vote. The director subject to removal shall be given at least seven (7) days' written notice of the meeting at which removal will be considered and shall have the opportunity to address the Board before the vote.

### Section 4.8 — Emergency Removal

The Board Chair may convene an emergency meeting with twenty-four (24) hours' notice to consider immediate removal of a director when the director's continued service poses an immediate threat to the Corporation's legal compliance, tax-exempt status, or organizational integrity. Emergency removal requires {{ amend_quorum_prose }} (excluding the director subject to removal). The removed director shall receive written notice within twenty-four (24) hours and may petition the full Board for reinstatement at the next regular meeting.

### Section 4.9 — Resignation

A director may resign at any time by delivering written notice to the Board Chair or the Secretary. The resignation is effective when the notice is delivered unless the notice specifies a later effective date.

### Section 4.10 — Quorum

{{ board_quorum_prose | capitalize }} shall constitute a quorum for the transaction of business. The act of a majority of the directors present at a meeting at which a quorum is present shall be the act of the Board, except where a greater vote is required by statute, the Articles of Incorporation, or these Bylaws.

### Section 4.11 — Regular Meetings

The Board shall hold regular meetings at least **[frequency, e.g. quarterly]**. The Board Chair shall set the date, time, and manner of each regular meeting. Notice of regular meetings shall be given to all directors at least seven (7) days in advance via **[your organization's designated communications platform]** or another method reasonably calculated to provide actual notice.

### Section 4.12 — Special Meetings

Special meetings of the Board may be called by the Board Chair or by any two (2) directors. Notice of a special meeting shall be given to all directors at least forty-eight (48) hours in advance via the same method described in Section 4.11. The notice shall state the date, time, manner, and purpose of the meeting.

### Section 4.13 — Remote Participation

Directors may participate in any meeting by telephone, video conference, or other electronic means through which all participants can simultaneously communicate with each other. A director participating remotely shall be deemed present in person for purposes of quorum and voting.

### Section 4.14 — Action Without Meeting

Any action required or permitted to be taken at a meeting of the Board may be taken without a meeting if all directors then serving consent in writing to the action. Written consent includes electronic consent transmitted via {{ mr_term }} approval on this repository (see `GOVERNANCE.md`), or any other verifiable electronic means the Board designates. Consent is effective when the last director signs or transmits consent. Actions taken by written consent have the same force and effect as a unanimous vote at a duly held meeting and shall be filed with the minutes of the Board.

### Section 4.15 — Electronic Voting

{{ mr_term | capitalize }} approval on this repository is valid written consent for Board actions as described in `GOVERNANCE.md`. [If your organization also uses a separate electronic-voting platform, name it here and describe how its records relate to the {{ mr_term }} record — see `GOVERNANCE.md` Section 7, "Integrity Rules."] The Secretary shall preserve electronic voting records as part of the permanent corporate records.

### Section 4.16 — Compensation

Directors shall serve without compensation. Directors may be reimbursed for reasonable expenses incurred in connection with their duties upon approval by the Board in accordance with the Corporation's Delegation of Authority Policy.

### Section 4.17 — Parliamentary Authority

The rules contained in the current edition of Robert's Rules of Order, Newly Revised shall govern the proceedings of the Board and its committees in all cases to which they are applicable and in which they are not inconsistent with these Bylaws, the Articles of Incorporation, or applicable law.

