## Article V — Officers

### Section 5.1 — Officers Designated

The officers of the Corporation shall be a Chair of the Board ("Board Chair"), a Vice-Chair, an Executive Director, a Secretary, and a {{ treasurer_title }}. The Board may create additional officer positions as it deems necessary. One person may hold two or more offices, except that the Board Chair and the Secretary may not be the same person.

### Section 5.2 — Executive Director

The Executive Director serves as the chief operating officer of the Corporation and manages its day-to-day operations, programs, and personnel under the direction of the Board. The Executive Director shall have authority to:

(a) Execute contracts, agreements, and other instruments on behalf of the Corporation in accordance with the Delegation of Authority Policy;
{%- if federal_grants_focus %}
(b) Certify proposals and serve as the Corporation's authorized organizational representative for federal grant submissions;
(c) Interact with contracting officers, grants management officers, and program officers on behalf of the Corporation;
(d) Manage compliance functions, including those required by federal grants, 2 CFR Part 200, and sponsor-specific terms and conditions;
{%- endif %}
(e) Supervise all employees, consultants, and volunteers of the Corporation;
(f) Report to the Board at each regular meeting on the operations and activities of the Corporation.

The Executive Director shall be an ex officio member of all standing committees.

### Section 5.3 — Board Chair

The Board Chair shall preside at all meetings of the Board, exercise general oversight of the affairs of the Corporation, and perform such other duties as the Board may assign. The Board Chair shall ensure that the orders and resolutions of the Board are carried into effect. The Board Chair serves as chairperson of the Governance Committee.

### Section 5.4 — Vice-Chair

The Vice-Chair shall, in the absence or disability of the Board Chair, perform the duties and exercise the powers of the Board Chair. The Vice-Chair shall perform such other duties as the Board Chair or the Board may assign.

### Section 5.5 — Secretary

The Secretary shall be responsible for the records of the Corporation, including minutes of all Board meetings, voting records, and official correspondence.{% if ai_agents_enabled %} The Board may delegate specific secretarial functions to an AI agent system operating under documented instructions, subject to Article XII. When secretarial functions are delegated to an AI agent, a human officer designated by the Board shall review and approve all official records before they are entered into the permanent record.{% endif %} The Secretary or the designated human officer retains ultimate responsibility for the accuracy and completeness of all corporate records.

### Section 5.6 — {{ treasurer_title }}

The {{ treasurer_title }} shall oversee the financial affairs of the Corporation, including budgets, financial reports, and tax filings. [If the Board delegates fiscal-custodian duties to an outside firm, name that arrangement here and cite the resolution authorizing it — do not hardcode a vendor name directly into these Bylaws, since vendors change independent of the Corporation's identity.] The {{ treasurer_title }} or the Board Chair shall review and approve all financial reports before they are presented to the Board or filed with regulatory agencies.

### Section 5.7 — Election and Term

Officers shall be elected annually by the Board of Directors at the first regular meeting of each calendar year, or at such other time as the Board determines. Each officer shall serve until a successor is elected and qualified, or until the officer's earlier death, resignation, or removal.

### Section 5.8 — Elections

Officers shall be elected by majority vote of the Board of Directors at the annual meeting or at any regular meeting where an officer vacancy exists.

### Section 5.9 — Removal of Officers

Any officer may be removed by a majority vote of the Board of Directors at any regular or special meeting. Removal of an officer shall not affect the officer's rights, if any, under any contract with the Corporation.

### Section 5.10 — Vacancy

A vacancy in any office shall be filled by the Board of Directors for the unexpired term.

### Section 5.11 — Official Communications

**[Name your organization's official board communication platform, if any.]** is the Corporation's official board communication platform. All official Board notices, correspondence, and communications shall be transmitted via that platform unless these Bylaws or a Board resolution specifically authorize an alternative method.

---

## Article VI — Executive Committee

### Section 6.1 — Composition

The Executive Committee shall consist of the elected officers of the Corporation (Board Chair, Vice-Chair, Secretary, and {{ treasurer_title }}) and the immediate Past Chair, if any. The Board Chair shall serve as chairperson of the Executive Committee. The Executive Director shall serve as an ex officio, non-voting member.

### Section 6.2 — Authority

The Executive Committee may act on behalf of the Board between regular meetings on matters that require timely action, except that the Executive Committee may not take any action reserved to the full Board under {{ incorp_statute }}, these Bylaws, or a Board resolution.

### Section 6.3 — Reporting

All actions taken by the Executive Committee shall be reported to the full Board at the next regular meeting and entered into the minutes.

---

## Article VII — Committees

### Section 7.1 — Standing Committees

The following standing committees are established by these Bylaws. The Board may establish additional standing committees by resolution. Each standing committee shall have a charter approved by the Board specifying the committee's purpose, authority, composition, and reporting obligations.

### Section 7.2 — Governance Committee

The Governance Committee is chaired by the Board Chair and is responsible for oversight of corporate governance, bylaw compliance, and organizational integrity, including reviewing proposed bylaw amendments and maintaining the Corporation's whistleblower reporting channel.

### Section 7.3 — Board Development Committee

The Board Development Committee shall be responsible for identifying, recruiting, and nominating candidates for Board membership, and for planning and delivering director orientation and continuing education.

### Section 7.4 — [Additional Standing Committee]

[Add any additional standing committees your organization requires — e.g. a Planning Committee, a Grant/Development Committee — with the same purpose/composition/authority structure as above.]

### Section 7.5 — Ad Hoc Committees

The Board may create ad hoc committees at any time for any purpose. Ad hoc committees shall be dissolved upon completion of their assigned tasks or by Board resolution.

### Section 7.6 — Committee Composition

Unless otherwise specified in these Bylaws, each committee shall consist of at least two (2) persons appointed by the Board. Committee members need not be directors. The Board shall designate a chair for each committee.

### Section 7.7 — Committee Authority

Committees may exercise only the authority specifically delegated to them by the Board or these Bylaws. No committee may amend the Articles of Incorporation; elect, appoint, or remove directors or officers (except as provided in Sections 4.7 and 4.8); adopt a plan of merger or dissolution; or take any action that requires a vote of the full Board under applicable law.

### Section 7.8 — Committee Quorum and Voting

A majority of the members of a committee shall constitute a quorum. The act of a majority of committee members present at a meeting at which a quorum is present shall be the act of the committee, except where these Bylaws require a higher threshold.

### Section 7.9 — Committee Meetings

Committee meetings may be held in person, by electronic means, or by written consent, following the same procedures applicable to the Board under Sections 4.13 and 4.14.

---

## Article VIII — Fiscal Year and Banking

### Section 8.1 — Fiscal Year

The fiscal year of the Corporation shall begin the day after {{ fiscal_year_end }} and end on {{ fiscal_year_end }} of the following period.

### Section 8.2 — Primary Bank

**[Name of primary financial institution]** is designated as the Corporation's primary financial institution. The Board may authorize additional depositories by resolution.

### Section 8.3 — Deposits

All funds of the Corporation shall be deposited to the credit of the Corporation in such banks, trust companies, or other depositories as the Board may approve.

---

## Article IX — Contracts, Checks, and Grants

### Section 9.1 — Contracts

The Board may authorize any officer or agent to enter into contracts or execute instruments on behalf of the Corporation. Such authority shall be exercised in accordance with the Corporation's Delegation of Authority Policy.

### Section 9.2 — Checks and Disbursements

All checks, drafts, orders for payment, and other disbursements of Corporation funds shall be authorized in accordance with the Delegation of Authority Policy and the fiscal procedures the Board establishes.

### Section 9.3 — Gifts and Grants

The Board or the Executive Director may accept, on behalf of the Corporation, any contribution, gift, bequest, devise, or grant for the general purposes or any special purpose of the Corporation, provided such acceptance is consistent with the Corporation's tax-exempt status{% if federal_grants_focus %} and, for federal awards, in compliance with 2 CFR Part 200 and applicable sponsor terms and conditions{% endif %}.

### Section 9.4 — Current Board Signatories

Authorized signatories for the Corporation's financial accounts and official documents are designated in `board-roster.md`, as maintained and amended through the Board's governance process. The Board may modify authorized signatories by resolution at any regular or special meeting.

---

## Article X — Conflict of Interest

Directors, officers, and key employees of the Corporation shall comply with the Corporation's Conflict of Interest Policy (`policies/conflict-of-interest.md`). The Conflict of Interest Policy, as adopted and amended by the Board from time to time, is incorporated by reference into these Bylaws. Each director and officer shall annually execute a disclosure statement as required by that policy.

---
{% if federal_grants_focus %}
## Article XI — Federal Grant Compliance and Export Control

### Section 11.1 — Federal Grant Compliance

The Corporation shall develop and maintain all policies, procedures, and internal controls required for federal grant eligibility, including but not limited to those required by 2 CFR Part 200 (Uniform Guidance), agency-specific regulations, and applicable federal statutes. All governance and compliance policies shall be adopted by the Board and maintained in this repository. The Executive Director shall ensure that the Corporation's systems and processes satisfy all applicable federal requirements.

### Section 11.2 — Export Control

The Corporation shall comply with all applicable export control laws and regulations, including the International Traffic in Arms Regulations (ITAR), the Export Administration Regulations (EAR), and sanctions administered by the Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC), to the extent applicable to the Corporation's activities. Board members, officers, employees, consultants, and any personnel with access to controlled technical data shall comply with the Corporation's Export Control Policy as adopted by the Board.

### Section 11.3 — Audit Committee

The Board shall establish an Audit Committee consisting of at least two (2) directors who are independent of management. The Audit Committee shall oversee the Corporation's financial reporting, internal controls, and compliance with 2 CFR 200 Subpart F (Single Audit requirements) once the Corporation's federal expenditures exceed the threshold set by 2 CFR 200 Subpart F.

---
{% endif -%}
{% if ai_agents_enabled %}
## Article XII — AI Agent Authorization

### Section 12.1 — Authorization

The Board of Directors authorizes the use of AI agent systems for operational and governance functions of the Corporation, including agenda preparation, minutes drafting, transcription, financial reporting, voting-record management, document drafting, and such other functions as the Board may approve.

### Section 12.2 — Governance Framework

AI agents shall operate under documented instructions and in accordance with the Corporation's AI Governance Policy for Board Operations (`policies/ai-governance-board.md`) and the hard rules in `CLAUDE.md`. Each agent's scope, workflow, permitted data sources, and output requirements shall be specified in its instructions.

### Section 12.3 — Human Oversight

No AI agent output shall become an official record of the Corporation until a responsible human officer has reviewed and approved it. AI-generated drafts shall be clearly marked as drafts until approved. The responsible human officer retains ultimate accountability for the accuracy, completeness, and legal sufficiency of all official records. **No AI agent has authority to cast a vote, approval, or written consent on behalf of a director or officer** — see `CLAUDE.md` for the full prohibition.

### Section 12.4 — Board Chair Control

The Board Chair may modify, suspend, or expand any AI agent's operating authority at any time. The Board Chair's authority under this section may be delegated to other officers by written notice.

### Section 12.5 — Authorized Platforms

The Board Chair may authorize AI platforms and tools for use in Corporation operations without requiring a formal amendment to these Bylaws. Such authorizations shall be noted in the next meeting's minutes.

---
{% endif -%}
## Article XIII — Indemnification and Insurance

### Section 13.1 — Scope

To the fullest extent permitted by {{ incorp_statute }} and consistent with the Corporation's Articles of Incorporation, the Corporation shall indemnify any person who was or is a party, or is threatened to be made a party, to any threatened, pending, or completed action, suit, or proceeding — whether civil, criminal, administrative, or investigative (including actions by or in the right of the Corporation) — by reason of the fact that the person is or was a director, officer, employee, or agent of the Corporation, against expenses (including attorneys' fees), judgments, fines, and amounts paid in settlement actually and reasonably incurred in connection with such action, suit, or proceeding.

### Section 13.2 — Advancement of Expenses

Expenses incurred in defending any action, suit, or proceeding covered by Section 13.1 shall be paid by the Corporation in advance of the final disposition of such action, suit, or proceeding upon receipt of an undertaking by or on behalf of the director, officer, employee, or agent to repay such amount if it is ultimately determined that the person is not entitled to be indemnified by the Corporation.

### Section 13.3 — Directors' and Officers' Insurance

The Board shall use reasonable efforts to obtain and maintain directors' and officers' (D&O) liability insurance in amounts and on terms the Board considers appropriate.

### Section 13.4 — Non-Exclusivity

The indemnification and advancement of expenses provided by this Article shall not be exclusive of any other rights to which a person seeking indemnification or advancement may be entitled under any agreement, vote of disinterested directors, or otherwise, and shall inure to the benefit of the heirs, executors, and administrators of such a person.

---

## Article XIV — Amendments

### Section 14.1 — Amendment by Board Vote

These Bylaws may be amended, repealed, or restated by {{ amend_quorum_prose }} at any regular or special meeting. Written notice of any proposed amendment, including the text of the proposed change, shall be given to all directors at least seven (7) days before the meeting at which the amendment will be considered. The Governance Committee may review proposed amendments and provide recommendations to the Board, but the Board vote is final and not subject to committee approval or veto.

### Section 14.2 — Emergency Amendment

In exigent circumstances requiring immediate bylaw modification to maintain legal compliance or tax-exempt status, the Board Chair may call an emergency meeting with twenty-four (24) hours' notice. Emergency amendments require {{ amend_quorum_prose }}. Emergency amendments shall be reviewed at the next regular meeting and may be ratified, modified, or repealed by the Board at that time.

---

## Article XV — Dissolution

### Section 15.1 — Procedure

The Corporation may be dissolved in accordance with the procedures set forth in {{ incorp_statute }}.

### Section 15.2 — Distribution of Assets

Upon dissolution of the Corporation, after paying or making provision for the payment of all liabilities, the Board shall distribute the remaining assets of the Corporation exclusively for the exempt purposes of the Corporation, or to one or more organizations that are organized and operated exclusively for charitable, educational, scientific, or other purposes qualifying under Section 501(c)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code, as the Board shall determine, consistent with the Corporation's Articles of Incorporation. No part of the assets shall be distributed to any director or officer of the Corporation.

